 are  african-american compared to  caucasian women and  post-menopausal african-american women  compared to  caucasian women so there's a higher  prevalence of basal-like breast cancer  in african-americans and especially  young african-americans  so  this gets to the other part of our grant  we um have community partners and we  partnered with the center for  environmental health and susceptibility  at unc as part of our grant  with the b cert program  and  in collaboration with  our bee surf group we also have a  community  advisory committee and we work on  outreach materials and  i'm going to show you a little bit about  what we've done there so what we're  hoping to do is translate some of our  findings in the lab through our  epidemiologic findings and findings in  our human studies with melissa truster  and our mouse findings in my lab into  humans so how do we  you know get this puzzle of individual  risk and population risk to help someone  understand their risk  so one thing we've done is a focus group  study and this is in collaboration with  um  marlin alicock misha graves kathleen  gray and melissa troster so um this is  something is a focus group study looking  at young african-american women and  their perspectives on breast cancer and  the findings were really shocking so  young african-american women perceived  breast cancer to be an older white  woman's disease  there was little to no knowledge of  subtypes  they wanted more relevant health  communications  and they we actually performed a second  focus group um with health care  providers and asked a lot of the same  questions and it was actually very  surprising to see  um that there wasn't a better knowledge  of the subtypes  in healthcare providers and that  manuscript is in  submitted in in preparation and  submitted  so um the last thing i want to talk  about  is  something that we've done is really  exciting and i hope that you all can go  to this website and i'm going to  push that to you 